fixes:
In `update` (reports.service.js, around line 421), keep the stored slot when the report already has a running schedule and its timing is unchanged. `fireSlot` reads the current recipients and formats, so the due slot then goes to the edited list.

```js
const fields = await scheduleFields(client, body.schedule, before.report_key, { active, now });
const sameTiming =
  canonical(fields.recurrence) === canonical(before.recurrence) &&
  fields.starts_on === before.starts_on &&
  fields.run_time === before.run_time;
if (before.report_schedule_id && before.active && sameTiming && before.next_run_at)
  fields.next_run_at = before.next_run_at;
if (!sameSchedule(fields, before)) scheduled = fields;
```

When the timing does change, recomputing from now is a defensible product choice. If the owner wants a due old slot honoured even then, keep `before.next_run_at` whenever it is <= now. Add an integration case in tests/integration/home-reports.test.js "the scheduler": with the slot due, PATCH the same rule with a different recipient list, then assert that the tick returns {due: 1} and that `fireSlot` makes runs for the new list.

Keep the ids tied to position, because HOME_STAGING_ROWS must stay a fixed list of every id the file can write. Keying by org_seat_id would make those ids depend on the database and break that list.

Make the slug fixed per position too, so an id always has the same slug. At line 215, replace `slug: \`stg-${slugOf(seat.title)}\`` with `slug: \`stg-open-seat-${i + 1}\``. The number at line 211 already depends only on position. The title, department and description can keep following the seat.

Optionally, for a position that no longer has a vacant seat, have the run close the row it wrote earlier instead of leaving it open. For example, after the loop in writeForwardRows: `UPDATE vacancy SET status = 'closed' WHERE vacancy_id = ANY($1) AND status = 'open'` with the open-seat ids that were left out.

Add an integration case to tests/integration/home-staging-seed.test.js:
- run the Home seed;
- `UPDATE org_seat SET status = 'closed'` on the first vacant seat by title;
- run the seed again;
- expect it to resolve, and expect VAC-STG-01 to carry the remaining seat's title.

Stop computing the manifest from ROWS:

1. In seeds/staging/lib/forward-rows.js, keep a frozen literal map of every id the seed has ever written, by table. When an owning milestone removes its rows from ROWS, the ids stay in that literal. HOME_STAGING_ROWS is built from the literal, in DELETE_ORDER.
2. Change the unit test at tests/unit/home-staging-seed.test.js:85-88. ROWS ids must be a subset of the manifest, and the manifest may hold tables that no longer have rows. Drop the `ids.length > 0` check, or apply it only to tables still in ROWS.
3. In each DATABASE_SCHEMA "Read by Home" note and in home.md §11, add a sentence saying the same PR updates:
   - DELETE_ORDER and the literal, which keeps the ids;
   - tests/unit/home-staging-seed.test.js;
   - the partner, budget or vacancy expectations in tests/integration/home-staging-seed.test.js;
